# Line endings are pinned, because in this project they are not cosmetic.
#
# A .ccert is content-addressed: its sha256 is its identity, and the
# verifier hashes the raw bytes. A CRLF conversion changes those bytes and
# therefore the digest, so a repository cloned on a machine with autocrlf
# on would present certificates whose digests no longer match the files
# beside them — a failure that looks like tampering and is only a newline.
#
# So: every text file in this tree is LF, on every platform, regardless of
# a contributor's git config. `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and
# checks out LF. The certificates go further with `-text`, which forbids any
# conversion at all — the stronger guarantee where every byte is identity.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Certificates: byte-exact, never converted. The sha256 is the identity.
*.ccert   -text

# Negative-vector expectations and notes are LF text. They were once `-text`
# too, which protected their bytes but also let a CRLF, once committed, stay
# committed — the drift could not heal. `text eol=lf` normalises them on the
# way in, so the canonical LF repairs itself. Their bytes are not an identity
# the way a .ccert's are; the .expect substring match tolerates the newline.
*.expect  text eol=lf
*.why     text eol=lf

# Source that the verifier or producer hashes or emits verbatim.
*.rs      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf

# Batch files are the one place CRLF is correct: some Windows shells
# mishandle LF-only .bat. These are not hashed by anything.
*.bat     text eol=crlf

# Never let anything touch a compiled artefact.
*.wasm    binary
*.zip     binary
